## Implementation Details & Features

### Orbital Mechanics
The physics engine accurately models celestial mechanics allowing for stable orbits around planets and moons. Players can take advantage of gravitational slingshot maneuvers by exploiting the inverse-cube relationship of gravitational forces. The engine calculates proper orbital velocities using the formula v = √(G*M/r) for any given radius around a celestial body. This creates authentic space combat opportunities where players can precisely position their ships to take advantage of gravitational assists.

### Performance Optimizations
The engine handles computational efficiency by profiling with the performance_profiler tool and optimizing calculations. When simulating multiple objects, gravitational forces only need to be calculated between them - distant objects have minimal effect due to the rapid falloff in the inverse-cube relationship F = G(m₁m₂)/r³. The implementation will only recalculate gravitational forces when necessary, reducing CPU usage.

### Modular Design
Both capital ships and small fighters are treated the same in terms of physics calculations, as the engine uses mass and position to calculate actions. This allows for diverse spacecraft sizes all behaving according to the same physical laws. Planets and stars also follow correct gravitational principles, providing a realistic space environment where the force decreases rapidly with distance.

### Integration with Game
The system interfaces with game rendering APIs to place objects properly in space based on their computed positions. Any collision detection or side effects can be managed through game callbacks when objects come into contact with each other. Time freezing features allow for strategic decision-making where players can pause and plan their next move without real-time pressure.

